  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Logan Webb held Detroit to one run over eight innings. The Giants tied it in the seventh. And then left the rest on the bases.",
    "what_this_means": "This is what inconsistency looks like from the inside. Webb gave them everything they needed. A clean slate, a chance to build. And the offense scraped together seven hits and fourteen strikeouts. They had eight chances with runners in scoring position. They converted zero of them. In the fourth inning, with two on and two outs, nothing came of it. In the eighth, with three runners on and the game still tied, they struck out again. That's not bad luck. That's a locked door at exactly the moment it needed to open. Detroit scored twice in the tenth, and by then the window had closed. Webb's performance made a one-run game possible. The bats made it impossible to win.",
    "what_to_watch": "Hayden Wesneski takes the mound tonight for Houston with a 3.86 ERA. A pitcher the Giants lineup has seen film on and a test of whether the offense can reset after leaving everything in Oracle Park.",
